NEW={
'hoobuy-product-spreadsheet-links-guide': {'title':'Hoobuy Spreadsheet & Product Links: Find, Check and Save',
'description':'Use a Hoobuy product spreadsheet to shortlist shoes and clothing, check seller links, compare variants and save a useful QC and shipping record.',
'body':'''<p>A Hoobuy spreadsheet is useful when it helps you return to an exact product, seller and variant. A long list of attractive photos is less useful if its links are broken, its prices exclude important costs or its quality-control examples belong to a different item. Start with a short, traceable shortlist and expand it only after the first few entries pass a basic check.</p>
<p>This guide covers product discovery: the searches often described as Hoobuy links, finds, clothing spreadsheets and shoe spreadsheets. If you already placed an order and need to record dispatch, warehouse intake or parcel IDs, use our separate <a href="/hoobuy-spreadsheet-guide">order-tracking spreadsheet and CSV template</a>. Keeping discovery and fulfilment in separate sheets makes both easier to maintain.</p>
<h2>Start with the product you can describe</h2>
<p>A useful search combines a product type with a detail that changes the decision. For shoes, that might be a model, size system or intended use. For a hoodie, use the cut, fabric weight if stated, colour or garment measurements. For a jacket, include the shape and whether you need information about lining or packed bulk. Broad searches are a starting point, but they cannot establish that a listing matches your requirements.</p>
<p>Open the <a href="/products">product discovery page</a> to search the linked catalogue. The catalogue is an external website, not Hoobuy checkout and not a promise of availability. Copy the original seller listing when the destination provides it. If you cannot identify the source or selected option, keep the entry as an unverified lead instead of treating the card as a purchase-ready recommendation.</p>
<h2>Build a spreadsheet with decision fields</h2>
<table class="matrix"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to save</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Source and review date</td><td>Exact listing URL and the date opened</td><td>A shared link or listing can change</td></tr>
<tr><td>Selected option</td><td>Model, colour, size and quantity</td><td>A low headline price may belong to another option</td></tr>
<tr><td>Price record</td><td>Displayed currency, amount and included costs</td><td>Product cost is only part of the budget</td></tr>
<tr><td>Size evidence</td><td>Seller chart and relevant measurements</td><td>Letter sizes are not consistent between sellers</td></tr>
<tr><td>QC reference</td><td>Link, date, exact variant and visible limitations</td><td>A reference photo is not evidence of your future item</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decision</td><td>Keep, clarify or reject, with a short reason</td><td>Prevents an attractive image from replacing a missing fact</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Use one row per meaningful option. If two sizes have different measurements or prices, separate them until you decide. Avoid copying account details, addresses, payment records or private order identifiers into a publicly shared spreadsheet. A discovery sheet can remain useful without exposing the information needed to identify a buyer.</p>
<h2>Check a Hoobuy link before placing an order</h2>
<p>Read the destination hostname and page title before entering any account information. A link collection can send you through several websites, and similar names do not establish common ownership. Use the official service address you already trust for account access. On the seller page, compare the product title, photographs, variant selector and seller identity with the spreadsheet row. A familiar thumbnail alone is not enough.</p>
<p>If a link no longer works, search by the exact product description and compare the replacement carefully. Record a replacement as a new source rather than silently overwriting the old seller. A visually similar product can have a different size chart, construction, package size or returns process. Do not assume that a changed URL preserves every fact in the previous entry.</p>
<p>Some pages need a selected option before displaying a price. Save that option with the amount and currency. If the destination shows a price range, your budget should use the amount for the intended variant. If the price cannot be confirmed, mark it as unknown; a blank or unknown field is more honest than a plausible estimate presented as current.</p>
<h2>Compare shoes, clothing and accessories differently</h2>
<p>For shoes, record the stated size system, seller size chart, model and whether the retail box is part of your intended package. A shoe length shown in a photograph is useful only when the method is clear. An outsole measurement and an internal fit measurement answer different questions. Treat buyer comments as context and compare the exact version whenever possible.</p>
<p>For hoodies, T-shirts and jackets, compare a garment you already own with the seller's stated measurement method. Chest width laid flat is not the same as body chest circumference. Shoulder position, sleeve method and overall length can also differ. Save the chart itself or its values, not only a conclusion such as “size up.” The <a href="/hoobuy-qc-photos-measurements-guide">QC photo and measurement guide</a> explains how to ask for evidence after an item reaches the warehouse.</p>
<p>For bags and accessories, note dimensions, included parts, closures and rigid sections. For electronic goods, identify the specification and any battery or plug information, then check the live shipping restrictions before ordering. A product being available in a catalogue does not establish that a route can carry it to your destination.</p>
<h2>Separate reference QC from your own QC</h2>
<p>Public QC examples can help you decide what to inspect, but they represent a particular item at a particular time. They do not prove what a seller will supply next. Check whether the reference matches the source, variant and approximate date. If those links are missing, use the photos only to form a question, such as which label or measurement you need to see.</p>
<p>Once your item is stored, match its warehouse identifier to your order and review its own photographs. Save visible concerns before approving a parcel. Photographs can show colour, quantity, labels, visible damage and measurements; they cannot establish authenticity, hidden construction, durability or guaranteed fit. Do not describe a listing as “QC verified” without saying exactly which item and which visible checks the evidence covers.</p>
<h2>Budget beyond the spreadsheet price</h2>
<p>Create separate columns for the selected item price, any displayed domestic delivery amount, service charges shown in the account, optional services and international shipping. Leave unconfirmed amounts explicitly unconfirmed. Currency conversion estimates should have a date and rate; they should not replace the amount payable in the actual checkout currency.</p>
<p>Shipping also depends on destination, eligible route, parcel weight and dimensions. Two products with similar purchase prices can lead to different packed shapes. A rigid shoebox or bulky jacket may change the calculation. Use the <a href="/shipping-guide">shipping calculator preparation guide</a> to collect inputs, then obtain a current route result from the official estimator. Avoid a universal “shipping per kilogram” figure that ignores the actual route.</p>
<h2>Turn the shortlist into a controlled order</h2>
<p>Before ordering, choose one exact variant, confirm that the listing is still available, review the size evidence and write down unresolved questions. If a critical field remains unknown, resolve it before paying. Save the source and option again at the moment of purchase because a spreadsheet entry reviewed earlier may already be stale.</p>
<p>After submission, move the confirmed selection into the private order ledger. Keep the discovery row as the original research record, and connect it to the private order using your own reference. Follow the <a href="/hoobuy-order-status-purchase-dispatch-qc-storage-2026">purchase, dispatch, QC and storage guide</a> for the operational stages. This avoids mistaking a useful shopping list for proof that an order has been purchased or dispatched.</p>
<h2>Maintain a smaller, more useful list</h2>
<p>Review frequently used rows first. Mark broken links and uncertain prices, remove unsupported claims and record the last date each source was actually checked. Keep a rejected entry when its reason would prevent a repeated mistake, but move it out of the active shortlist. The quality of a Hoobuy spreadsheet comes from accurate options, useful measurements and clear decisions, not its row count.</p>
<p>For your next session, select three candidate products, complete the source, variant, measurement and budget fields, then compare the gaps. The candidate with the fewest unresolved questions may be more useful than the cheapest headline price. That is a practical way to turn product links into an informed purchase process without pretending a catalogue can guarantee the result.</p>'''
},
'hoobuy-qc-photos-measurements-guide': {
'title':'Hoobuy QC Photos: Find, Read and Request Measurements',
'description':'Match Hoobuy QC photos to the right warehouse item, inspect visible details and request useful measurements before packing or a return decision.',
'body':'''<p>Hoobuy QC photos are most useful when you know which decision each image should support. A photograph can help confirm a visible label, quantity, colour, measurement or condition. It cannot promise authenticity, hidden material quality, long-term durability or how an item will fit your body. Begin by matching the photos to the exact warehouse item, then ask only for the missing evidence that could change your next action.</p>
<p>This is an independent inspection method, not a description of guaranteed free services. Photo locations, available requests and charges can change. Check the current item record and the options shown in your account before requesting additional work. Never assume that a photo package seen in another buyer's account is included in yours.</p>
<h2>Find the correct item before judging the images</h2>
<p>Open your order and its connected warehouse or stored-item record. Look for the photos attached to that item rather than relying on a generic product picture. The exact interface wording may vary. If the record has no images, establish whether the item has actually completed intake and whether a photo service is available. A seller dispatch notice or domestic delivery scan does not itself mean the warehouse has photographed your item.</p>
<p>Compare the source listing, selected variant, quantity and identifiers. An agent order ID, warehouse item ID and parcel ID refer to different records. Keep their relationship visible using the <a href="/hoobuy-order-id-warehouse-item-id-parcel-id-guide-2026">identifier guide</a>. Where one order arrives in several packages, confirm which photographed item belongs to each package before reporting a missing piece.</p>
<p>Download or save the images you need together with their date and item reference. Preserve an unedited copy before making annotations. Publicly sharing a screenshot can expose names, addresses, tracking numbers or account information; crop or redact those details in any copy you share while retaining the original privately for support.</p>
<h2>Inspect in a consistent order</h2>
<table class="matrix"><thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Useful evidence</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Identity and quantity</td><td>Overall views, labels and included pieces</td><td>A similar shape can hide a different variant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Selected option</td><td>Colour, size tag, model and visible details</td><td>Lighting and a tag alone do not prove fit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Visible condition</td><td>Close views of the specific area of concern</td><td>Hidden defects may remain invisible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Measurements</td><td>Readable scale, endpoints and laid-flat method</td><td>Different methods produce different values</td></tr>
<tr><td>Packaging</td><td>Box condition, parts and protective material</td><td>Item photos do not show the final packed parcel</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Checking identity first prevents wasted effort on a detailed measurement of the wrong item. Checking quantity early matters when sets, pairs or accessories are involved. Once those basics match, inspect the areas that matter for the specific category rather than applying an identical checklist to every product.</p>
<h2>Read clothing measurements carefully</h2>
<p>For a T-shirt or hoodie, decide which measurements you need: chest width, garment length, sleeve length or shoulder width. Use a garment that already fits as your comparison and measure it in the same way. If your reference chest width is 54 cm laid flat and the seller's chart states 108 cm circumference, those values may describe the same nominal dimension, but only if the methods match. Do not compare 54 directly with 108 as though both were flat widths.</p>
<p>A useful measurement photograph shows the whole relevant section, where the tape starts, where it ends and the numbers clearly enough to read. Fabric should lie naturally without unexplained stretching or folds. A curved tape, hidden zero point or angled camera can make an apparently precise number unreliable. Ask for a clearer method before treating a small difference as conclusive.</p>
<p>Record the observed value and any uncertainty separately. For example, “chest approximately 54 cm, tape start partly hidden” is more useful than “size correct.” Decide your acceptable measurement range before reviewing the result, based on your own garment and preferences. This guide does not supply one universal tolerance for every fabric or clothing type.</p>
<h2>Adapt the check to shoes and accessories</h2>
<p>For shoes, inspect both shoes, the visible size labels, sole pattern, left-right consistency and any obvious damage. If length information matters, specify what should be measured. The outside sole length does not equal internal usable length, and an insole may not be removable. Ask whether the requested method is available rather than instructing someone to damage or dismantle the item.</p>
<p>For bags, check overall dimensions, handles or straps, closures, visible seams and included accessories. For items with rigid parts, check obvious deformation and whether protection will be needed during shipping. These observations help with acceptance and packaging decisions, but a photograph cannot demonstrate load capacity or extended use.</p>
<p>Keep the seller's reference beside your item photos. Differences in camera angle and lighting can alter the appearance of colour and shape. Where a colour difference is decisive, request a neutral, well-lit image if that option exists. Describe the uncertainty instead of assuming either that every difference is a defect or that every difference is caused by lighting.</p>
<h2>Write one precise request for missing evidence</h2>
<p>A useful request identifies the item, the exact view or measurement, the method and the reason it affects your decision. For example: “For this warehouse item, please show the chest width laid flat from underarm to underarm, with both tape endpoints visible. I need to compare it with my reference garment before deciding whether to keep it.” Include the actual item reference only in the private support or service request.</p>
<p>For a visible concern, identify the area rather than asking for “better QC.” A request for a close view of the left cuff seam or the heel of the right shoe is actionable. If several details are missing, combine compatible requests into one clear list and check any displayed charge before submitting it. Repeated broad requests can create more images without resolving the original question.</p>
<p>Save the request wording and response with the item record. If a requested check cannot be performed, keep that limitation visible and decide whether the remaining uncertainty is acceptable. Silence, an unavailable service or an unrelated image should not be recorded as a passed inspection.</p>
<h2>Choose between acceptance, clarification and a return enquiry</h2>
<p>Accept the visible evidence only when identity, quantity and your decision-critical checks are resolved. If a missing image could change the result, request clarification before packing. If the available evidence shows a mismatch or unacceptable condition, preserve the original order details and photos, then review the current return or exchange options. Availability, eligibility and deadlines depend on the actual transaction and current terms.</p>
<p>The <a href="/hoobuy-returns-exchanges-after-warehouse-qc-2026">returns and exchanges guide</a> explains how to organise that evidence. Avoid assuming that a warehouse rejection automatically creates a refund, or that a photo request pauses a deadline. If the account displays a time limit, record it and ask for clarification while that option is still available.</p>
<h2>Carry the decision into the parcel stage</h2>
<p>Item QC and parcel packing answer different questions. Accepting a garment's visible condition does not establish that the final box has suitable protection or the dimensions used for a shipping quote. Record which items are accepted, which remain unresolved and which must be excluded from the parcel. Then write packaging instructions that match the accepted contents.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/shipping-guide">shipping preparation guide</a> to collect the final weight and outer dimensions, and the <a href="/hoobuy-packaging-box-removal-reinforcement-moisture-guide-2026">packaging guide</a> to compare protection choices. Removing packaging solely to reduce volume can create a different risk. The decision should follow the item's shape and protection needs, not a generic rule applied to every order.</p>
<h2>Keep an inspection record you can explain</h2>
<p>A compact record contains the item reference, selected option, photo date, observations, unresolved questions, request history and final decision. Link it to the <a href="/hoobuy-spreadsheet-guide">private order-tracking worksheet</a>. Use factual observations such as “two pieces visible” or “label shows size M,” and keep conclusions such as “acceptable for my use” separate.</p>
<p>This separation makes later support requests clearer. It also prevents a reference photo from becoming an unsupported claim that an entire seller or product line has been verified. The useful result of QC is a documented decision about the item in front of you, with its limits understood before international shipping makes correction more complicated.</p>'''
},
'hoobuy-process-pending-guide': {
'title':'Hoobuy Process Pending: Check the Order Before Acting',
'description':'Investigate a Hoobuy process pending order using payment, purchase and message records. Identify the missing action without guessing processing times.',
'body':'''<p>If a Hoobuy order shows “Process Pending,” first establish what has actually happened to that specific order. A payment record, a purchase record and seller dispatch are different events. The status text alone cannot establish that a seller has been paid, that an item is in stock or that a parcel exists. Open the order details and work from dated evidence before deciding whether to wait, answer a message or contact support.</p>
<p>This guide is an independent troubleshooting method. It does not assign one guaranteed processing time or one universal cause to that label. Account wording and procedures can change, and only the current order record or a response about that order can confirm the unresolved step. Keep the exact label when contacting the service rather than translating it into a different status.</p>
<h2>Separate a pending process from a confirmation request</h2>
<p>Start with the precise words displayed in the account. “Process Pending” and “Pending for Confirmation” should not be treated as interchangeable. If the order contains a request for you to accept a changed variant, amount or condition, inspect that request directly and follow the <a href="/hoobuy-pending-for-confirmation-status-2026">pending-for-confirmation guide</a>. Waiting without reading an explicit decision request may leave the actual blocker unresolved.</p>
<p>If no buyer action is shown, do not invent one from a forum screenshot. Save the current label, its timestamp if provided, the order reference and the visible history. Check the order's own message area and the relevant account notifications. A useful investigation distinguishes “no action shown” from “I have not opened the order details.”</p>
<h2>Reconstruct the last verified event</h2>
<table class="matrix"><thead><tr><th>Evidence</th><th>What it supports</th><th>What still needs checking</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Payment entry</td><td>A recorded transaction or balance movement</td><td>Whether it is settled and assigned to this order</td></tr>
<tr><td>Purchase confirmation</td><td>A recorded purchasing step</td><td>Seller acceptance, selected option and dispatch</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seller dispatch details</td><td>A stated outgoing package</td><td>Carrier acceptance and the correct domestic number</td></tr>
<tr><td>Carrier event</td><td>A scan in a particular tracking system</td><td>Warehouse matching and intake</td></tr>
<tr><td>Warehouse item record</td><td>An identified stored or processing item</td><td>QC, acceptance and international parcel submission</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Write a short timeline using only events you can point to. For example, a payment entry followed by the same pending label is evidence of payment and the displayed state; it is not proof of seller dispatch. If the order history contains a purchase event but no tracking number, the missing evidence belongs later in the sequence. The <a href="/hoobuy-order-status-purchase-dispatch-qc-storage-2026">order-status guide</a> maps these handoffs.</p>
<h2>Check payment without paying twice</h2>
<p>Match the order amount, currency, payment reference and transaction state. Distinguish a pending payment authorisation from a completed account transaction when the payment provider or account makes that distinction. If funds appear in an account balance, confirm whether the order record actually uses them. Do not assume that a bank entry and an order entry describe the same completed step.</p>
<p>If the order displays an additional balance, open the explanation and determine whether it concerns the item, domestic delivery, a changed option or another stated amount. Use the <a href="/hoobuy-paid-order-outstanding-balance-2026">outstanding-balance guide</a> to reconcile the records. Never make a second payment solely because a status label has not changed. An unresolved discrepancy is a reason to clarify the transaction, not evidence that paying again will fix it.</p>
<h2>Check the selected item and seller information</h2>
<p>Reopen the saved listing and compare the exact size, colour, model and quantity with the submitted order. A changed listing or unavailable variant can be relevant, but it is not proof of the cause of this pending status. Record what changed and ask whether it affects the order. Avoid silently substituting a new link or changing several options at once, because that can make the original request harder to trace.</p>
<p>If the order message asks for a replacement choice, verify its price and description before responding. If it reports a purchasing failure or cancellation, move to the <a href="/hoobuy-purchase-failed-order-cancelled-status-2026">purchase-failed and cancelled-order guide</a>. A different outcome needs its own evidence trail, including any balance or refund record, rather than being treated as an unusually long pending stage.</p>
<h2>Decide whether the next action belongs to you</h2>
<p>Use the live message and available controls to identify the next action. A clear request to confirm an option belongs to the buyer. A missing explanation about a transaction may require account support. A purchase event without dispatch evidence calls for a question about seller dispatch. Once the carrier has accepted a package, investigate the domestic tracking record instead of repeatedly asking about the original purchase stage.</p>
<p>If there is no explicit request and no contradictory evidence, record the last check and consult any current processing information the service publishes. Treat that information as a reference for the current service, not a personal deadline guaranteed by this site. Another buyer's completion time can differ because the seller, goods, order state and date differ. Do not turn an anecdote into a rule for every order.</p>
<h2>Send a support request that can be answered</h2>
<p>Include the order reference, exact status text, time of your latest check, last verified event and one focused question. A useful structure is: “This order shows Process Pending. The latest confirmed event is the payment recorded at the time shown in the attached account screenshot. I cannot see a purchase confirmation or a buyer action request. Please confirm the current purchasing stage and whether anything is required from me.” Replace the example with your actual evidence.</p>
<p>Attach only the relevant order and payment views through the service's private channel. Hide unrelated transactions and never include passwords, one-time codes or full payment-card details. Keep the original images privately. If an existing conversation already covers this order, add new evidence there when practical so the history remains connected instead of creating several conflicting accounts of the same issue.</p>
<p>Save the reply with its date. Distinguish an acknowledgement that the request was received from an answer identifying the next step. If the response asks for information, provide exactly that information and preserve the exchange. If a stated follow-up point passes without the promised update, reply with the earlier reference and the missing result rather than restarting the entire story.</p>
<h2>A worked example with two different decisions</h2>
<p>Consider two illustrative orders with the same pending label. The first contains a message asking the buyer to approve a different colour. The useful action is to compare the proposed option, price and conditions, then respond through the available control. Waiting for a generic processing estimate does not resolve that decision.</p>
<p>The second has a recorded payment but no purchase event and no request visible to the buyer. The useful action is to save the timeline and ask whether purchasing has begun and whether any buyer input is missing. The label alone does not justify saying the seller is slow, the warehouse is delayed or the payment failed. These are different hypotheses, and the next record should identify which stage actually needs attention.</p>
<h2>Avoid duplicate orders and premature parcel tracking</h2>
<p>Do not create a replacement order merely to test whether the first one will move faster. If both proceed, you may have two purchases to resolve. Before cancelling or reordering, inspect the options and consequences currently shown for the original transaction. A cancellation request is not the same as a completed cancellation, and a refund request is not the same as funds returned.</p>
<p>Likewise, an agent order number is not automatically a courier tracking number. International tracking generally belongs to a later parcel record. Use the <a href="/hoobuy-tracking-number-domestic-international-guide-2026">domestic and international tracking guide</a> to avoid searching a carrier website with an unrelated identifier. When evidence shows seller dispatch, continue with the <a href="/hoobuy-seller-sent-warehouse-not-received-2026">seller-sent handoff guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Close the issue with a recorded next state</h2>
<p>When the order changes, save the new event and verify that it answers the original question. A purchase confirmation closes a purchasing uncertainty; it does not prove warehouse receipt. Carry the order forward in the <a href="/hoobuy-spreadsheet-guide">order-tracking worksheet</a> and keep the source, variant and identifiers connected. The goal is a clear next step supported by evidence, rather than a guessed explanation for a short status label.</p>'''
}}

def make_article(slug,d):
    # A minimal source document; build.py supplies shared navigation and metadata.
    return f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>{escape(d['title'])}</title><meta name="description" content="{escape(d['description'],quote=True)}"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/styles.css"></head><body><main class="wrap" id="content"><article class="article" id="main-article"><p class="eyebrow">Practical buyer guide</p><h1>{escape(d['title'])}</h1><p class="meta">Published: October 12, 2026</p><div class="disclosure"><p>HooBuys.net is an independent editorial website, not Hoobuy. Check current account messages, service options and official terms before making a purchase or shipping decision.</p></div>{d['body']}<section class="sources"><h2>Sources and method</h2><p>This article provides an editorial decision method. The <a href="https://hoobuy.com/help-center" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official Hoobuy help centre</a> and your current account are the places to verify service-specific procedures. Examples are illustrative; they are not records of actual customer orders.</p><p><a href="/editorial-policy">Editorial policy</a> · <a href="/articles">All articles</a></p></section></article></main></body></html>'''

for slug,d in NEW.items(): (EN/(slug+'.html')).write_text(make_article(slug,d))

